      "grade": "Correct",
      "source_grade": "Correct",
      "raw_html": "<div class=\"question\" id=\"question-72319\"><div class=\"questionHeader\"><span class=\"questionNumber\">3.</span><a class=\"questionKP\" href=\"/topics/1979#6102\">Identifying Type II Regions</a><span class=\"helpButton\" questionid=\"72319\">?</span></div><div class=\"questionText\"><p>Which of the following is a type II region in two-dimensional space?</p>\n</div><div class=\"answerDetails\"><div class=\"questionDifficulty difficultyEasy\">E</div><span class=\"answerCreated\">Wed, Oct 14th @ 3:24 AM</span><span class=\"answerDetailsSeparator\">|</span><span class=\"timeElpased\">Elapsed: 0:17</span><span class=\"answerResult\"><span style=\"color: green;\">Correct</span></span></div></div>"
    },
    {
      "dom_id": "question-73534",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14199341/assets/c316b62d5c1a08c6513413fd87c36c6feb26fb632709909a9c9f761d0806c4af.png)\n\nWhich of the following represents the shaded region above as a type II region in two-dimensional space?",
      "worked_solution": "A type II region can be written as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,a\\le y\\le b,\\,g(y)\\le x\\le f(y)\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are continuous functions.\n\nTo express the given region as a type II region, we first rewrite all of the given curves as functions of $y:$\n\n$$\ny=-2(x-3)\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{y}{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe function $x=-\\sqrt{y}+2$ is already written as a function of $y.$\n\nNotice that the region lies between the horizontal lines $y=1$ and $y=3.$ So, we have $1\\le y\\le 3.$\n\nWe draw a horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left function $g(y)$ and the right function $f(y).$ A horizontal line should intersect the region in a single interval only for it to be a type II region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14199341/assets/fb05eec73599c1beb8dd588c478208315f6edaf10af999115eb29fa10d5601e9.png)\n\nThe horizontal line enters the region through the lower function $x=-\\sqrt{y}+2$ and leaves through the upper function $x=-\\frac{y}{2}+3.$ So, we have $-\\sqrt{y}+2\\le x\\le -\\frac{y}{2}+3.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 3,\\,-\\sqrt{y}+2\\le x\\le -\\frac{y}{2}+3\\}.\n$$",
